WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.080
Hi, welcome to in Limone where we talk about the Constitution, Justice and Criminal Law.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.840
Today we're privileged to have John Carriacou, I hope I pronounced your name right.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:15.840
You did exactly right.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:16.840
Thank you.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.880
You're practicing it all morning and messing it up.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:26.480
John is a best-selling author of a number of books and those include The Reluctant

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:34.480
Spy, My Secret Life, and the CIA's War on Terror from 2010, The Convenient Terrorist, Abu Zubeda.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:41.080
Zubeda, thank you, and The Weird Wonderland of America's Secret Wars.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:48.280
I love that subtitle, and then 2017, Doing Time Like a Spy, How the CIA Taught Me to

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:53.000
Survive and Thrive in Prison, and I also understand you're an advocate of prison

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.360
reform after your delightful experience.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.360
Very much so.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.960
I think pretty much everybody is when they come out of prison.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.200
Our system is utterly broken.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.880
It's utterly, utterly broken.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.760
You were in Pennsylvania, right?

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.280
Pennsylvania, Central Pennsylvania Police called FCI Loretto.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.720
Federal Correction Institute Loretto.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:12.720
Correct.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.040
What was it like there?

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.320
It was a shock at first.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.840
My attorneys at Sentencing asked the judge to send me to a minimum security work camp,

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.480
and the prosecution had no objection.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.760
So she said, okay, minimum security work camp.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:34.360
And what you do when you're assigned to a work camp, you literally just drive up there

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:40.120
and knock on the door and say, I'm John Kiriakou, I'm here to turn myself in.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.120
So I did that.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.560
Well, all camps are connected.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:49.120
They're usually across the street from an actual prison, because if there's a riot

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.840
or a lockdown, the guys from the camp do the laundry, do the cooking, do the cleaning,

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:54.840
that kind of thing.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:01.440
Otherwise, if you're at a camp, there are no bars on the windows.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.560
The doors aren't locked.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:03.760
There are no fences or anything.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.340
You're free to come and go as you please.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.960
You're on your honor, not too abscond, of course, and most guys work in town.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:15.320
There's a university there, and you sweep the floors or whatever.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:21.040
So I drove up there, and with a whole gang of people, like my cousin and his son,

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:27.320
two of my attorneys, two of my 11 attorneys, a documentary film crew.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.320
And I knocked on the door, and they said, oh, you got to go across the street to the

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.920
prison first, and they'll process you, and then they bring you back over here.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.440
I said, OK.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:41.360
So I went across the street to the prison with the double fences with the concertina

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.640
wire and the guard towers and the whole nine yards.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.640
And I said, I'm John Kiriakou.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.160
I'm here to turn myself in.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.640
And so I had to go through a metal detector.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.720
And then they start bringing me, they brought me outside, and then around to the back of

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:56.720
the prison.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.040
And I said, no, no, I'm supposed to be at the camp across the street.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.040
And the guard laughed at me and said, not according to my paperwork, you're not.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.360
And I told myself, take it easy.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.280
There's nothing you can do.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.200
If you raise a ruckus, they're going to put you in solitary, so I didn't say anything.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.200
It took me five days to get access to a phone.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:27.560
And I called my lead attorney and I said, hey, they put me in the actual prison with the pedophiles

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.560
and the drug kingpins and the mafia dons.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.320
What do I do?

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.760
And he said, my God, well, we can file a motion, but it'll be two years before we

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.680
even get a hearing and you'll be home by then.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:44.360
He said, I'm sorry, buddy, you're going to have to tough it out.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:50.560
And so I decided that very moment you're trained for this.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:58.240
I've lived in far, far worse places on earth than Loretto, Pennsylvania.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:04.480
And so I just kicked myself in the butt and said, all right, just deal with it.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.480
Wow.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:06.680
So that's what I did.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:11.880
And for people who don't know, DOJ controls the Bureau of Prisons.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.720
That was a tough lesson that I learned.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.800
DOJ controls the Bureau of Prisons and the Bureau of Prisons answers to no one.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.480
They don't care what the judge says.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.880
They don't even care what their own prosecutors say.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.800
BOP sends people to whatever facility BOP wants to send people.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.360
I was fortunate in that they didn't send me to a communications management unit.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:42.240
What they ended up doing, well, what ended up happening and what resulted in my third

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:52.960
book was I had a group of about 600 people who had signed up for a mailing list that

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.040
my lawyer had set up.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.520
And as I was leaving for prison, my lawyer said, when you get comfortable, just drop

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:05.080
me a line and I'll send it around to the people on the mailing list just so that

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.080
they know you're okay.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.040
I said, all right, I waited about six weeks until I got my bearings.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:20.440
And then I wrote what I called a letter from Loretto because I love the idea of Martin Luther

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.680
King's letter from Birmingham jail.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.720
So I wrote a letter from Loretto and it was sort of a day in the life, right?

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:34.680
Well, I didn't know that my lawyer was close friends with Ariana Huffington.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:41.440
And so she put it like as a banner headline on the Huffington Post and it went crazy.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.640
I got more than two million hits over the course of the next several days.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.520
And I ended up giving interviews to everybody from Jake Tapper who drove to the prison

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:59.880
to interview me to NPR and Playboy and The Economist and The Atlantic Monthly and

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:06.480
you name it, they immediately clamped down on my access to the outside world.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.080
So they moved me into what they called a modified CMU.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:18.000
So there was a five day delay on my incoming and outgoing emails, five days.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.440
And they listened to my outgoing calls live rather than just to have them taped

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.960
and if they felt like looking at or listening to them that they would listen to it.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:32.920
It was all live monitored and I speak Greek and I speak Arabic.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.840
So sometimes like when if I'm talking to my kids or I was talking to my aunt or whatever

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.680
I would speak Greek and they're like, no, no, no, you can't do that.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:40.680
It's like, why?

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:43.400
Because you're too stupid to speak Greek.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:49.360
I got in trouble once for for calling one of the guards a Nimrod on the phone.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.000
It was a private conversation with my wife and I got called down to the lieutenants

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.400
office.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.880
And when you hear your name, you know, report to the lieutenants office.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:03.320
You're like, okay, guys, it's been good to know you.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.240
Take care of my stuff.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:08.920
And you go to solitary and then eventually you're sent to a higher security prison

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:11.000
because I called the guy Nimrod.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.160
And as soon as I went down to the lieutenants office, this lieutenant who really was like

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:19.520
the biggest asshole of anybody in the prison, he said, do you think I'm a Nimrod?

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.040
You know, first he said, can I talk to you man to man, which means I'm going

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.480
to swear at you and I don't want you to report me for it.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.040
I said, sure, you can talk to me man to man.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:32.400
He goes, you think I'm a Nimrod?

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.960
And I said, I don't know.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:36.360
Maybe you are.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:37.360
Maybe you're not.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.120
I don't know you, but the other guy's a Nimrod.

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:43.520
And it just got worse from there.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:47.040
Yeah, you can't, you can't peak their insecurities.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:48.040
No.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.080
And I would say that.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.760
You know, I would, I would mock them.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:56.480
You know, the, the irony is that half the time they're so stupid, they didn't realize they

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.480
were being mocked.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:58.480
Yeah.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:00.040
That was always amazing to me.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:01.040
Yeah.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:07.120
One of them, I was being taken to an outside, I had broken my pinky finger, so it's being

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:11.720
taken to an outside medical consultation.

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:17.800
And one of them said that he had gotten passed over for a GS4.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:20.000
And I said, like it's my business, right?

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:25.760
I go GS4, how do you buy food?

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:26.760
And he goes, why?

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:27.760
What were you?

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:32.440
I said, I was a 15 and I could barely buy food.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.600
And they're like asshole.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:38.480
And then for, for people who don't know what the GS ranking system is, that's government

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:39.480
services.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:40.480
Yeah.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:46.360
If you're, if you're below, if you're a GS5 or below, like you're expected to

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:50.280
be able to scrawl your X at the top of the paper, right?

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:51.280
Yeah.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:52.280
Yeah.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.680
You have to have a bachelor's degree to be a seven.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:59.920
And that's something that people, I don't, it's incredible to me.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:02.680
I don't think people understand about American prison systems.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:07.680
And I didn't fully comprehend until I was in a Canadian prison where they've got

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.680
unionized guards who make six figures.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.200
And I was like, why is everybody so nice here, right?

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.440
And I feel like in all the federal institutions I've been in America, you know, this is generalization.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:19.160
There's good people and bad people everywhere.

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:22.920
But there's this, it's almost like it's the only place in their life that they have

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:23.920
any power.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:24.920
Yeah.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:25.920
That's it.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:26.920
And they own you.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:27.920
And all the penniness comes out.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:33.480
And compare your, first of all, what you just said, Joe Bonanno, the long time head

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:37.520
of the Bonanno crime family said exactly the same thing in his book, Man of Honor.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:40.560
He was arrested and held for something like six months in Canada.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.720
He said they couldn't have been any more lovely to him.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:47.440
But in the United States, it's a whole different class of people.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:53.880
There's a book called In Defense of Flogging written by a professor at John Jay College

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:57.080
of Criminal Justice in New York.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:00.040
His name is Peter Moscow.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:08.800
And he says that the Bureau of Prisons is really nothing more than an employment agency for otherwise

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:17.440
unemployable, rural, uneducated white men. That's why our prisons are in, you know, no man's land,

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:26.440
in the mountains, out in the prairie. They're not near anything that can account for employment.

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:35.400
And so they tend to attract people who either flunked out of the local police academy or couldn't

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:41.000
get into the local police academy in the first place, people who left the military and have nowhere

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:48.440
else to go, people who have no education. They live in farming areas but aren't farmers. And where

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:53.080
else are you going to go? You become a prison guard. And it's the only place, you know, I said

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:57.800
this one time and to a guard and he was furious that I said it. But I said, you know, one of the

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:04.760
things that I think you guys all have in common is in seventh grade, you were that skinny little kid

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:11.080
who got bullied. And you've only realized now that you have these jobs that you can bully

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:17.640
somebody else. And there's nothing that they can do about it. And it hurt, it stung because

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:25.000
it was true. Yeah. Yeah. And if there's that whole American punitive mindset, and it's just

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:32.120
not, that was a big wake up for me when I first started visiting clients in jail is just how

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:38.120
petty they could be. And the, because people have just this view of it that it's oh yeah,

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:41.560
the prison guards, it's all like running like you think it's going to be on TV. And it's

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:45.480
just a, you know, and the federal prisons compared to the state prison. That's what

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:50.280
everybody says. I've never seen a state prison. But that's what everybody says that the feds are

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:55.560
head and shoulders above what state prisons are. I can't even imagine it. And did they because

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:59.080
I've had, you know, clients who've been like politically controversial and stuff like a lot of

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:05.720
hackers. And it always struck me that they, they like throw them in the shoe, which is

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:10.600
basically solitary, just because of who they are. And did you get thrown in the shoe at

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:16.760
all? Do you think your celebrity protected you? Oh, that's a great question. And this was something

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:22.840
that that reared its head over and over and over again. And what it came down to, first of all,

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:30.760
I was never thrown in the shoe. Oh lucky man. They threatened me constantly. And then I was

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:36.200
sort of adopted by the Italians when when I was there. And when I say Italians, I mean,

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:43.560
named Gambino, Bonanno, Lucchese, you know, you get the idea. And so I sat at the Italian table

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:48.280
in the cafeteria. I socialized with the Italians. I ate dinner with the Italians every night.

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:54.360
So one of the Italians said to one of the guards, where are you going for the next quarter?

00:12:54.360 --> 00:13:01.320
And he said, I'm in central one unit, which was where I lived. And the Italian said,

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:08.760
oh, my buddy John lives in central one. And the guard said, the CIA guy? Yeah. And the guard said,

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:17.880
and this guard had a reputation for being like, particularly bad, big bully. He was the only

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:23.400
guard in the entire prison that wore a stab vest underneath his, his uniform, because he was afraid

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:29.080
of being shanked. And he had a piece of masking tape that covered up his name. He was he was

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:35.640
known only as blue. We didn't know what his real name was. So he said, oh, I never mess with that

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:42.760
CIA guy. And the Italian said, really, why? And he said, because that's all I need. I mess with him.

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:48.600
And I go out to my car at the end of my shift and CNN's out there waiting for me. Yeah. And I said

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:53.960
exactly. And none of them ever messed with me. None of them. Your celebrity protected you.

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:59.640
It did. It protected me. Yeah. And I never expected any of that. I never planned for any of that.

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:05.800
The honest to God's truth of the matter was that if they had let me go to the camp,

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:12.120
like I was supposed to go, I would have slept, swept floors for 23 months, like they would have

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:18.200
expected. I wouldn't have said a word to anybody. I would have just quietly read books.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:22.840
And then I would have gone home. But they're the ones that had to make a big production

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:28.280
out of the whole thing. They'd love to make a production out of stuff. And it's a lot about,

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:33.320
I think, like publicity drives a lot of stuff. And very much so. And I'll tell you another thing,

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:38.360
because I think this is an important point that you're making. Just out of curiosity one day,

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:42.840
because I was bored, I wrote a Freedom of Information Act request to the Bureau of

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:50.040
Prisons for everything they had on me, everything. And 95% of what came back was

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:55.640
just garbage. It was my medical records. It was my visitor's list, my phone call list,

00:14:55.640 --> 00:15:03.480
stuff like that. But either somebody was like a mental defect in the Bureau of Prisons Freedom

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:08.600
of Information Act office, or somebody in that office took pity on me, because they sent me

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:13.400
back several documents that were very clearly stamped in red at the top and the bottom of

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:23.000
the page. FOIA exempt, do not release to inmate. Oops. Yeah. And one of those things was a memo.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:29.880
It was an all hands memo to everybody in the prison the week before I arrived there.

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:37.720
And in these huge block letters, it said caution, inmate has access to the media.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:40.920
Wow. And that's really what it was all about.

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:47.880
You would think that if they were running things properly, and in our supposedly democratic

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:51.960
republic that we have that they wouldn't mind the media, because if you're doing it,

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:56.760
what do they have to hide, right? Well, they have to hide. It was incredible to me. And again,

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:05.960
the thought of contacting the media never occurred to me until they made it an issue.

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:10.200
Oh, you're not going to be in the camp. You're going to be in the real prison.

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:16.840
And then I said to on my very first full day there, I had to meet with my whatever they

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:24.680
call themselves your your case case worker or whatever it is. Yeah. And I said, listen, I've

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:28.840
got a I've got a bachelor's degree in Middle Eastern studies. I have a master's degree in

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:35.400
policy analysis. And I finished my PhD casework or I mean, coursework at UVA in international

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:41.480
affairs. So if you want me to teach, you know, GED class or whatever, I'm happy to do it.

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:49.160
And he screams at me, if I wanted you to teach a fucking class, I would ask you to teach a

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:56.360
fucking class. And they made me a janitor in the chapel. I was like, okay, that's fine.

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:59.000
Yeah, I know how to do that. I'm not going to help you run your prison.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:04.920
Yeah, there's that. That's an interesting like take on the or expression of American

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:11.400
anti intellectualism that resentment that I think part of that feeds is behind the Trumpism.

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:15.720
But I don't. What's the big deal? What's the big deal? What's the big deal? I'm offering to help

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:21.880
