Because I was doing things like...

00:41:58.000 --> 00:42:03.000
One of the interesting things to me about the Espionage Act is I was talking to Steve Ladik about it during this prosecution.

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:05.000
And I'm a little autistic.

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:07.000
I always need to know the basics of things, right?

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:09.000
And I'm a big con law guy.

00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:13.000
So I'm looking at the Espionage Act, and I can't see, you know,

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:18.000
supposedly in our United States Constitution it's supposed to be one of enumerated powers,

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:24.000
and Congress could only pass statutes that are explicitly listed in the Constitution.

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:26.000
Now the New Deal kind of blew that up, right?

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:29.000
But the first thing I always check is, okay, what gave Congress the authority...

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:30.000
Yes.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:31.000
...to pass this act?

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:32.000
And I can't find it in the...

00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:34.000
I can't find it in the case law.

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:35.000
So I asked Steve Ladik.

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:37.000
I said, Steve, you know, you're the expert in this...

00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:44.000
Can you just tell me, like, what authorizes Congress to pass the Espionage Act?

00:42:44.000 --> 00:42:46.000
And he couldn't give me a straight answer.

00:42:46.000 --> 00:42:52.000
So I'm like, oh, I want to go challenge the constitutionality of the Espionage Act.

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:57.000
And Daniel Hill's federal defenders, and I call them federal surrenders, told me I was crazy.

00:42:57.000 --> 00:42:58.000
Right?

00:42:58.000 --> 00:42:59.000
But I'm like...

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:00.000
You're not crazy.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:05.000
In fact, Daniel Ellsberg would agree with you 1,000%.

00:43:05.000 --> 00:43:08.000
This is something that he's been doing a lot lately.

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:15.000
He and I talked a lot when Jeffrey Sterling, the CIA whistleblower, was in prison.

00:43:15.000 --> 00:43:23.000
Dan really, really wanted Sterling to appeal beyond the court of appeals because Dan believes,

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:29.000
